DESCRIPTION:
  Config-file-gated command injection. The server must be started with
  --config pointing to a file containing exec_mode = 1 and exec_logging = 1
  for the vulnerable /exec endpoint to be reachable. A user-supplied URL
  query parameter flows directly into system() with no sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C2 — config file):
  mechanism:  key=value config file parsed by read_exec_config(); exec_cfg
              populated via fopen/fgets/sscanf; two fields (enabled + logging)
              must both be 1
  check:      exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.logging two lines above dispatch
              `if (exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.logging && strncmp(conn->url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: config file I/O → struct population → two-field guard; 3+ function
              hops from fopen to system()

  CONFIG FILE:
    --config /path/to/file   (required CLI arg to load settings)
    File format:
      exec_mode = 1
      exec_logging = 1

INPUT_FLOW (I1 — direct):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  conn->url (set by parse_request())
  path:       process_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() extracts the substring after "cmd=" via strchr()
              the raw string is passed directly to system()
  transforms: none — no urldecode(), no snprintf(), no copy

SANITIZATION (S1 — none):
  none

SINK:        system() in handle_exec() (darkhttpd.c, injected after generate_dir_listing)

CHANGED FILES:
  darkhttpd.c — 5 hunks:
    1. global:              added struct exec_config and exec_cfg instance
    2. usage():             added --config entry
    3. read_exec_config():  new function parsing key=value file via fopen/fgets/sscanf
    4. parse_commandline(): added --config branch calling read_exec_config()
